# Test conventions

Full text: `AGENTS.md` §5. These also apply to `#[cfg(test)]` modules inside `src/`.

## Names (auto-enforced)

`given_<state>_when_<action>_then_<observable>`. The part after the last `_then_` is
observable — `then_r1_includes_carry`, never `then_works` / `then_ok`.

## No control flow in a test body (auto-enforced)

No `if` / `else` / `for` / `while` / `loop` / `match` / `let … else` / `for_each`. Put the
table in a harness function that returns the failures and assert the list is empty.

```rust
// Bad — stops at the first failing case
#[test]
fn given_adc_suite_when_run_then_all_match() {
    for case in load_cases("arm/adc.json") {
        assert_eq!(run_case(&case), case.expected);
    }
}

// Good — every failing case shows at once
#[test]
fn given_adc_json_suite_when_each_case_runs_then_no_case_differs() {
    let failures = run_json_suite::<false>("arm/adc.json");
    assert_eq!(failures, Vec::<CaseDiff>::new());
}
```

## Every test asserts (auto-enforced)

`assert*!`, `prop_assert*!`, an insta snapshot, an `assert_*` helper, or
`#[should_panic(expected = "...")]`.

## What to test

- The failing test comes **before** the implementation; a `FIX:` carries its reproduction.
- Expected values come from `docs/hw/` / GBATEK / the ARM ARM — never from our own output.
- Determinism: two runs of the same ROM + input log give identical frame hashes and
  savestate bytes; save → load → continue matches an uninterrupted run.
- Edges: pipeline offsets, r15 writes, banked registers, interworking, unaligned access,
  open bus, mirrors, bank remaps, IRQ with IME=0, FIFO full/empty, timer cascade, DMA modes,
  scheduler ties.

## Golden frames and snapshots

Both screens are hashed. **Never regenerate a hash or bulk-accept snapshots to go green.**
A changed hash needs its reason in the PR. Commit `proptest-regressions/`.

## Coverage floor 90%

`cargo llvm-cov --fail-under-lines 90` runs in `./scripts/verify.sh` and CI. **Never lower
the floor or exclude files** — write tests that assert behavior.

## Nothing commercial, no network

Only our own `testroms/` (sources only — built ROMs are never committed) and synthetic
data. A local-only test needing a user's ROM reads `NINGE_USER_ROM` and is
`#[ignore = "needs a user-supplied ROM"]`.
